keep three cart faces, not eighteen

A GBA cart was OOM-killed mid-game on the handheld (exit 137, signal 9, no
core). slot was holding 22.1 MB of texture art at the time, and 7.2 MB of that
was eighteen cart faces at 312x337 -- fourteen for the current shelf and the
rest for the neighbouring shelves' visible spans -- on a device with about
60 MB free.

Upstream's FACE_AHEAD of 8 and FACE_KEEP of 12 are a GPU device's numbers: on
a shelf of fourteen carts, 12 is all of them, and shed_faces goes on keeping
them while a game is playing and no cart is on screen at all. Both become 1
here, so the resident set is the selected cart and its immediate neighbours,
and a neighbouring shelf keeps its selected cart rather than its whole span.

The two have to move together. What `face_wants` asks for is what `shed_faces`
has to keep, or the workers spend every frame building faces that are shed
before they are drawn; upstream can want a span and keep 12 because 12 is the
wider of the two, and here both are FACE_AHEAD.

The cost, asked for deliberately: the shelf draws up to SLOTS either side, so
carts past the kept three are flat label-coloured rectangles until a face
arrives. That is upstream's own placeholder -- it is what every cart looks
like for the first moment after a boot -- and it is the thing to look at on
the handheld before deciding whether 1 is the right number or 2 is.

Upstream's three tests for this encoded the old numbers, so they are rewritten
to the same assertions against the new ones rather than deleted.

